WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.115 --> 00:00:01.740
BENJAMIN SCHULER:
SAP systems are often

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.920
amongst the most critical parts
of a company's IT landscape,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.590
and therefore, they need to
be protected against downtime

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.780
to allow continuous operation
of the entire company.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.420
Google Cloud can help you
improve your SAP system's

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.090
uptime and provide you
with different options

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:20.770
for designing high-availability
architectures.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:21.370
Hi.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:22.660
My name is Benjamin Schuler.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.640
And in this video,
I'd like to tell you

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.410
a little bit about
the different options

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.650
that you have for designing
high-availability architectures

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:31.730
for SAP on Google Cloud.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.354
Let's take a look.

00:00:33.354 --> 00:00:37.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.120
Before looking at the
SAP-specific architecture

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.790
patterns, I want to highlight
some of the out-of-the-box

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:49.090
concepts that Google Cloud can
offer to improve the uptime

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:52.630
of any virtual machine running
on Google Compute Engine.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.150
Google Cloud is highly
available by design,

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.670
with a redundant
infrastructure of data centers

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.760
around the world that
contain zones designed to be

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.380
independent from each other.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.900
The blue dots on the
map indicate regions

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.250
that are available today.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.470
The white ones indicate
future regions.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:13.180
Zones usually have individual
power, cooling, network,

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.050
and control planes that are
isolated from other zones.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.400
If a single failure
event were to occur--

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.940
for example, a bulldozer
cutting through a network cable

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.830
during some nearby
construction work--

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.170
it would affect
only a single zone.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.570
As you can see, every
region in Google Cloud

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.970
provides at least
three isolated zones,

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:32.890
which enables our
customers to design

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.040
a high-availability architecture
across multiple zones

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.020
in the region of
their preference

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.960
without being tied to
selected parts of the world.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.970
Since all of our regions
offer multiple zones,

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.990
the first step towards a proper
high-availability architecture

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.990
in Google Cloud should be
to distribute your instances

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.050
across different zones
within the same region.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.910
Our high-performing
network connection

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:56.860
ensures that we
can provide latency

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.840
below one millisecond
from one zone

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.450
to another in the
same region, which

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.610
makes synchronous
replication between systems

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:07.583
a valid option without
keeping the users waiting.

00:02:07.583 --> 00:02:09.750
Now that we have learned
about the different regions

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.540
and zones, let's
look at some features

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.740
that we can offer thanks to
our virtualized infrastructure.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.510
When we need to
perform maintenance

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:22.290
on our infrastructure, Google
Compute Engine automatically

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.800
migrates your instance away
from the maintenance event while

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.970
trying to keep your application
running without any downtime.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.550
We call this feature
Live Migration.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:36.210
This happens without
any user interaction,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.320
and is a feature that we
provide free of charge.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.620
The migrated
instance is identical

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.870
to the original instance,
including the instance

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.710
ID, private IP address, and all
instance metadata and storage.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.890
Another handy feature that
comes with Google Compute Engine

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.340
is our automatic restart
of virtual machines.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.440
If your instance
is set to terminate

00:02:58.440 --> 00:02:59.940
when there is a
maintenance event,

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.480
or if your instance crashes
because of an unexpected issue,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.510
you can set up Compute Engine
to automatically restart

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.930
that instance.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.920
This setting is turned on by
default on all virtual machine

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.750
instances, and we
recommend that you

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.790
keep this feature activated.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.800
In combination with
a startup script,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.160
you can automate your SAP
system start after a crash

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.710
without any manual
user activity,

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:28.030
and therefore, reduce its impact
on the user significantly.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:30.850
In some cases, you might
already meet your availability

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:33.220
requirements by running
your systems on Google Cloud

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.820
infrastructure without the need
for redundant components, which

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:40.180
could save you both
time and money.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.670
When it comes to running
SAP HANA databases

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:45.370
in a high-availability
setup, the first mechanism

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:47.800
that comes to mind is
the built-in HANA system

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.270
replication.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:51.790
SAP provides this
functionality out of the box,

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:53.740
and offers a number
of replication models

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.100
so you can tweak it to
match your requirements.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.580
For synchronous replication,
SAP recommends network latency

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.430
below one millisecond between
the primary and secondary

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:05.090
database.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:07.780
This means that you can
deploy your two HANA instances

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.420
in two different zones
in any of our regions,

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.720
and protect your system
against a failure of one zone

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.520
while still meeting
the requirements to run

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.800
a synchronous replication
between both instances

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:20.300
in that same region.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.820
If you'd like to try such
a setup for yourself,

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.500
check out this
link, which provides

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.480
some additional
information, and also

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.510
a template for our own automated
infrastructure provisioning

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.880
service, which is called
Deployment Manager.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.670
This script will spin
up two HANA instances

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.760
in two different zones,
then configure HANA system

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.210
replication between them, and
also set up a Linux cluster

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:44.420
without any user
interaction, fully automated,

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.190
in less than 30 minutes.

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.250
Pretty cool, huh?

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.730
For scale-out HANA
systems, so a scenario

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:53.680
where multiple
nodes are connected

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.500
to form one combined
database, you

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.600
can deploy additional
standby nodes that

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.650
can take over in case one
of the worker node fails.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.980
As you can see in this diagram,
the data and log partitions

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:09.040
of each node are attached
to a single virtual machine

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.030
instance.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:14.590
In our example here, worker
node two is no longer available,

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:18.290
and the standby node
immediately takes over.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.980
The data and log partitions are
detached from worker node two

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.910
and mounted to the standby node.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:27.280
This is executed
by a storage plugin

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.800
that works together with the
SAP Storage Connector API.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.050
The standby node can now
function as a new worker node,

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.560
and once the failed worker
node is available again,

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.200
it will act as the
new standby node.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.840
With Google Cloud, you
also have the option

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.000
to run non-HANA databases.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.370
Check out our database
planning and deployment guides

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.510
behind this link.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.600
These databases have their
own built-in functionality

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.300
to provide a high-availability
architecture.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:59.740
For example, a warm standby
server with SAP ASE's Always On

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:05.080
option, or a high-availability
cluster of IBM's DB2 database.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.900
For your application
servers, the first components

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.360
that you probably want to
protect against downtime

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.090
are the central services.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:16.840
Similar to the database, these
present a possible single point

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:21.130
of failure, and therefore,
should be protected.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.770
You can choose to run
the different services

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:27.010
on multiple instances, so
have one server running

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.170
the message service
and another server

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.870
running the enqueue service.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.630
If your message
service instance fails,

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.670
the instance running
the enqueue service

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.950
can take over with the
latest log information

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:43.730
and continue to allow users
to work with your SAP system.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:46.280
For such a setup, we
recommend that you

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.960
distribute the
different instances

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.760
across multiple zones.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.640
One of the benefits of running
your SAP system on Google Cloud

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.210
infrastructure is the ability
to provision new servers

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.610
in a matter of seconds
and to automate

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:01.580
the entire installation process.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:04.910
With this flexibility, you can
get new application servers

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:07.410
ready for your users
as they are needed.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:09.080
And by running
multiple instances,

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.230
they can immediately log onto a
different server if one of them

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:14.120
fails.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.130
OK, let's wrap up.

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.870
As a general
statement, it can be

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:20.960
said that moving an SAP system
from on-premises hardware

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.110
to Google Cloud infrastructure
can improve the availability

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:27.290
through built-in concepts
of the virtualization layer,

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:30.230
like Live Migration
and Host Auto-Restart,

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:32.000
and therefore,
improve the uptime

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.130
without any additional
configuration.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.110
Downtimes caused by
hardware failures

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.820
can be reduced or
even eliminated

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.990
without requiring any
customer activity.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:44.570
For the database
layer, various concepts

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:46.700
exist depending on
the database system.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.200
Typically, these
concepts are either

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.720
based on a regular copy
of the database logs

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.590
to a standby system or a set
of database instances running

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:57.620
in some sort of synchronous
replication mode.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:59.900
For such a setup, the
two database instances

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:01.700
should be placed
in different zones

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.400
within the same Google
Cloud region for the highest

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.980
level of isolation while
still meeting requirements

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:08.920
for low latency.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.090
And finally, on the
application layer,

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:14.170
the SAP NetWeaver architecture
can provide end users

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:16.360
with multiple
application servers

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.940
to ensure the failure
of one instance

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.700
does not block all users
from accessing the system,

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.320
and that those who
lose connection

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.190
can log on via a different
server immediately.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:30.250
With the flexible infrastructure
in Google Cloud and automated

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:32.409
deployment scripts, a
new application server

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:36.159
can be added on demand, and
placing multiple application

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:39.039
servers in a [? logon ?]
group can improve the uptime

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:42.270
of the application layer.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.010
I hope this brief
video has given you

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.130
a better idea about the various
high-availability architectures

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.110
on Google Cloud
and what they mean

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:50.890
for running your SAP workloads.

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:52.890
You can find a lot of
additional information

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.650
about SAP on Google Cloud
in our YouTube channel

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.698
and in the online documentation.

00:08:57.698 --> 00:09:00.240
Check out those links right next
to me and in the description

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.270
below.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:02.610
Thanks for watching.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:06.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

